---
import '../styles/global.css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
import BackButton from '../components/BackButton.astro';
import SleepCheckin from '../components/SleepCheckin.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout title="Sleep Check-in" description="Track your rest and see your sleep trends. Simple daily logging that stays on your device.">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="page__header">
      <BackButton />
      <h1 class="page__title">Sleep Check-in</h1>
      <p class="page__desc">Track your rest, see your trends</p>
    </div>
    <SleepCheckin client:load />
  </div>

  <article class="article-guide">
    <div class="article-guide__inner">
      <h2>Understanding Sleep Tracking and Daily Recovery Trends</h2>
      <p>
        Optimizing sleep begins with accurate self-awareness. However, in the era of wearable sensors and smart rings, many individuals find themselves bombarded with complex algorithmic scores, heart rate variability percentages, and sleep stage graphs. For many, this excessive data creates a modern psychological paradox known as <em>orthosomnia</em>: an unhealthy obsession with achieving flawless sleep metrics that paradoxically triggers nighttime performance anxiety.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Sleep Check-in</strong> tool takes a mindful, low-friction approach grounded in cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I). By logging subjective rest quality, morning alertness, and lifestyle variables directly on your device, you develop deep somatic awareness without the anxiety of automated grading.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <strong>The Two-Process Model of Sleep:</strong> Formulated by sleep researcher Dr. Alexander Borbely, sleep is regulated by two primary forces: <em>Process S</em> (homeostatic sleep pressure, driven by the buildup of adenosine throughout waking hours) and <em>Process C</em> (the circadian rhythm, governed by the suprachiasmatic nucleus). Understanding how your daily habits influence both forces is key to resolving persistent sleeplessness.
      </div>

      <h3>Key Scientific Concepts in Sleep Tracking</h3>
      <div class="guide-card-grid">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>Subjective vs. Objective Rest</h4>
          <p>Clinical sleep studies consistently demonstrate that how refreshed you feel upon waking is a stronger predictor of daytime cognitive focus and mood than estimated percentages of deep or REM sleep.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>Orthosomnia Prevention</h4>
          <p>High-tech sleep trackers often misclassify quiet wakefulness as light sleep, creating false panic. A subjective morning check-in focuses on your lived experience rather than sensor error.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>Circadian Regularity</h4>
          <p>Tracking your wake times reveals your circadian consistency. Maintaining a stable morning rise time anchors your biological clock and regulates natural melatonin production at night.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>How to Conduct an Effective Morning Check-in</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Wait for sleep inertia to clear:</strong> Do not evaluate your rest within the first 60 seconds of waking. Allow 15 to 30 minutes for morning adenosine clearance and grogginess (sleep inertia) to lift before rating your rest.</li>
        <li><strong>Rate your subjective energy:</strong> Evaluate how your body physically feels on a simple 1-to-5 scale. Focus on physical restoration, mental clarity, and ease of getting out of bed.</li>
        <li><strong>Note previous evening factors:</strong> Make a brief mental note of variables from the previous night: Was caffeine consumed after 2 PM? Was there late-night screen exposure? Did you engage in cognitive shuffling or breathwork before turning off the lights?</li>
        <li><strong>Identify multi-day patterns:</strong> Avoid obsessing over a single bad night. Sleep naturally fluctuates. Look instead for weekly trends to see which evening rituals consistently yield restful mornings.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="guide-disclaimer">
        <strong>Wellness Notice:</strong> The Sleep Check-in tool is a personal lifestyle tracking and self-reflection aid. It is not a clinical medical diagnostic device, polysomnography assessment, or medical evaluation for sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, or chronic clinical insomnia. If you suffer from chronic unrefreshing sleep, loud snoring, or daytime somnolence, please consult a board-certified sleep specialist.
      </div>

      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the Two-Process Model of sleep regulation?</summary>
          <p>The Two-Process Model shows that sleep timing is determined by the interplay between Process S (the homeostatic drive that grows stronger the longer you stay awake) and Process C (the 24-hour internal circadian clock that dictates alertness based on light exposure and time of day).</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why is subjective sleep quality often more useful than tracker scores?</summary>
          <p>Commercial fitness trackers use accelerometers and optical pulse sensors that estimate sleep stages through mathematical guesswork. Subjective ratings capture your actual physiological and cognitive recovery, which is the ultimate measure of restorative rest.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is orthosomnia and how does this tool prevent it?</summary>
          <p>Orthosomnia is a term coined by sleep researchers to describe anxiety caused by tracking devices. People become stressed when their sleep tracker shows a low score, which increases nighttime cortisol. Fall Asleep prevents this by using simple, calm, subjective check-ins with zero gamification or judgmental grades.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the single most important metric for better sleep?</summary>
          <p>Consistency of wake-up time. Keeping a regular wake time every day (even on weekends) sets your master circadian pacemaker, ensuring your body reliably produces melatonin at the same time each evening.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is my sleep check-in history stored on external servers?</summary>
          <p>No. All check-in history, notes, and ratings remain exclusively inside your web browser local storage. We have no backend databases, user tracking, or cloud synchronization. Your personal wellness data belongs entirely to you.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Navigation slot="nav" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 0.5rem 6rem;
  }

  .page__header {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .page__title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text);
  }

  .page__desc {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
